</div><p>Supports login with a handle and password.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--playing-through-irc" class="blog-single__content-title">Playing through IRC</h2>
<p>To play over IRC, run:</p>
<pre class="highlight"><code>M-x chess-irc</code></pre>
<p>It will connect to your configured IRC server, then ask for your opponent's nick. Your opponent needs to do the same on their end. No worries about privacy: the game uses private messages.</p>
<p>Note: this feature is part of my <a href="https://github.com/jwiegley/emacs-chess/pull/40" target="_blank">Pull Request</a>, so if you do not see it, you may need to update your <code>chess</code> version or build it from the repository.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--final-notes" class="blog-single__content-title">Final notes</h2>
<p>My configuration:</p>
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-elisp">(use-package chess
  :ensure t
  :config
  (setq chess-images-separate-frame nil) ; Display the board in the same frame
  (setq chess-images-default-size 100))  ; Piece size in pixels</code></pre>
<p>One last piece of advice: play against humans. The AI is quite easy, and while winning is fun, it is not much of a challenge. You will also get to meet more people that way. Checkmate!</p>
<p>I hope you enjoy your chess games inside Emacs as much as I do.</p>